LAYOUT:
  structure: |
    ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
    │                    Deep Space Background                            │
    │                    (gradient + nebula + noise)                      │
    │                                                                     │
    │    ┌───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
    │    │                                                           │   │
    │    │              CONSTELLATION NETWORK VIEW                   │   │
    │    │                                                           │   │
    │    │         ○ ────── ● ────── ○                              │   │
    │    │        /          \                                       │   │
    │    │       ○            ○ ←── Hero Node (centered)            │   │
    │    │                     \                                     │   │
    │    │                      ○                                    │   │
    │    │                                                           │   │
    │    └───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
    │                                                                     │
    │    ┌───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
    │    │            HERO TEXT PANE (Glass Effect)                  │   │
    │    │                                                           │   │
    │    │  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐│   │
    │    │  │  🔊  │  🎤  │  🔊  │  👀  │   ← Phase Strip          ││   │
    │    │  │ prompt│speak │voice1│voice2│     (icons)              ││   │
    │    │  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘│   │
    │    │                                                           │   │
    │    │  "I want to speak Italian with you"    ← Known text      │   │
    │    │                                                           │   │
    │    │  "Voglio parlare italiano con te"      ← Target (voice_2)│   │
    │    │                                                           │   │
    │    └───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
    │                                                                     │
    │              ⏮  ▶️  ⏭                ← Transport Controls       │
    │                                                                     │
    │    ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────  │
    │    Star Field (static distant stars)                                │
    └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

  layers:
    z_0: "Space gradient background"
    z_1: "Space nebula overlay"
    z_2: "Background noise texture"
    z_3: "Static star field"
    z_4: "Constellation network view"
    z_5: "Hero text pane (glass)"
    z_6: "Node hover tooltip"
    z_7: "Transport controls"
    z_8: "Overlays (session complete, consent prompts)"

## ============================================================================
## HERO TEXT PANE
## ============================================================================

HERO_TEXT_PANE:
  description: "Floating glass panel showing current phrase and phase"

  visual:
    background: "Frosted glass effect (backdrop-blur)"
    border: "Subtle belt-colored glow"
    position: "Lower third of screen, over network"

  modes:
    intro_mode:
      display: "Typewriter-style encouraging message"
      elements:
        prefix: "› (chevron)"
        message: "Typewriter text animation"
        cursor: "▌ (blinking block cursor)"
      triggers: "Item type is 'intro'"

    normal_mode:
      display: "Phase strip + known/target text"
      elements:
        phase_strip: "Horizontal 4-section strip with icons"
        known_text: "Known language text (always visible)"
        target_text: "Target language text (voice_2 only)"

## ============================================================================
## TILE ASSEMBLY & SCRIPT DISPLAY (June 2026)
## ============================================================================

TILE_ASSEMBLY:
  description: "Phrase decomposed into per-LEGO tiles (LegoAssembly.vue)"
  source: "packages/player-vue/src/components/LegoAssembly.vue"

  dual_script_model: |
    Every non-Latin-script course shows the NATIVE script as the primary glyph
    with the romanisation as an optional ruby line above each tile
    (block.romanText). The script toggle (ModeTray "Pronunciation") only shows/
    hides the ruby — it is NOT a switch between two scripts. Default on; the
    learner drops the crutch as recognition builds. Three tiling strategies are
    dispatched by script shape (utils/alignRomanToNative.ts):

  key_principle: |
    Tiling is ALWAYS derived from the ROMANISED side (the reliably-segmented one
    — word spaces or syllables). currentPhraseLegoBlocksRaw builds on romanised
    (useNative=false); currentPhraseLegoBlocks then produces the native-primary
    display per script. Lesson from the first cut: the NATIVE decomposition is
    unreliable for spaceless scripts (Japanese's is mostly ghost characters),
    whereas the romaji decomposes cleanly.

  strategies:
    authored_display_tiling:
      langs: "any course with course_practice_phrases.display_tiling (jpn_for_eng
              backfilled June 2026, ~99% of phrases)"
      how: "Strategy 0, authoritative. Popty stores authored tiles
            [{n: native, r: roman, salient}] per phrase, validated both ways at
            build time (roman = stored value sliced, native reassembles exactly).
            Carried additively on the cycle as displayTiling through every
            serving path (generateLearningScript select, toSimpleRounds,
            cycles/infplay-cycles endpoints + get_course_cycles_window RPC,
            bundle). currentPhraseLegoBlocks renders them verbatim when the
            joined n-values reassemble the displayed native phrase
            (whitespace-normalised); NULL or mismatch falls through to the
            runtime strategies below. SCRIPT_VERSION v9 invalidated cached
            scripts so the field appears without a content_version bump."
      priority: "Checked before all runtime strategies — the device segmenter
                 is now the fallback, not the primary path."
    mandarin_pinyin_word:
      langs: "zho"
      how: "buildWordTiles — tile by pinyin word; 1 hanzi = 1 syllable, each
            pinyin word consumes its syllable-count of hanzi (erhua 儿 +
            apostrophe). LEGO decomposition only marks salient tiles."
      validated: "zho_for_eng: 5436 tiled, salient located 100%."
    spaced_word_pair:
      langs: "kor, rus, ell, hye, ara, hin, ukr, fas, bul, heb (RTL: ara/fas/heb)"
      how: "buildWordPairTiles — native and romanisation are both whitespace-
            segmented and 1:1, so zip into one tile per word (native primary,
            romaji ruby). FINER than the LEGO decomposition (whose salient can be
            a whole clause). Salient from the salient LEGO's native text. Ruby
            LTR + bidi-isolated for RTL. Null on count mismatch → native-pair."
      validated: "word-parity 94% (ara) – 100% (rus/hin/bul/heb)."
    spaceless_device_segmented:
      langs: "jpn, tha (Thai)"
      how: "buildSegmentedTiles — the DEVICE segments the native sentence into
            words via Intl.Segmenter(locale,'word') (ICU dictionaries, ja/th),
            so tiling does NOT depend on the backend decomposition (which is
            39-69% ghost for these). Romaji per tile, in order of preference:
            (ja) alignJapaneseRomaji — forced-align the phrase romaji onto the
            segmented words using kana as anchors + kanji as gaps recovered from
            the stream; then positional phrase-romaji on count match; then the
            course native→romaji dict (legos+atoms); else native, no ruby (still
            parseable). Salient by char-offset. Old engines → nativeFromRomanTiles."
      validated: "jpn combined romaji coverage 97.6% tiles / 90% phrases; tha ~96%."
    latin:
      langs: "spa/fra/deu/… (no target_text_roman)"
      how: "hasRomanizedText=false → raw romanised tiling unchanged, no ruby."

  intro_glosses:
    rule: |
      (Tom 2026-06-07, v2: tiles must be IDENTICAL to regular phrases)
      Intro/debut show the known-language gloss UNDER the tiles — first
      presentation must let the learner match the pieces in both languages
      ("cosa|azul / thing|blue") — but the tiles and ruby themselves render
      EXACTLY as the LEGO appears in a regular phrase; the gloss is purely
      additive. attachIntroGlosses (LearningPlayer) wraps every word-tiling
      strategy's output on intro/debut only: component targets (union of
      componentsNative + components — a component whose roman form is NULL,
      e.g. zho 你, only exists in the roman list with native fallback text)
      claim contiguous tile runs, order-independently (DB returns component
      phrases in arbitrary order), longest target first. Each tile gets a
      glossGroup id; LegoAssembly's intro-gloss-groups template branch (a
      v-if BEFORE carriage/single-tile modes) renders runs as flush tile
      rows with the run's gloss centred underneath (.gloss-group-row /
      .gloss-group-tile). Structural particles keep their empty known
      (claimed run, no gloss); unmatched components simply don't gloss —
      tiles are NEVER merged or re-tiled. Single-tile LEGOs get the whole
      known text. Known text never shows outside intro/debut. The gloss
      line is RESERVED on every run (min-height, even when empty — e.g. a
      structural particle's silent gloss): the assembly bottom-aligns the
      groups, so a missing line would push that run's tile down by one
      gloss-height relative to its neighbours.

  typography:
    uniform_bold: |
      (Tom 2026-06-07) Tile text is ONE size + weight (600) everywhere — the
      old salient-vs-context split (smaller/muted non-salient text, softer
      non-salient chrome, hero known-text salient-substring emphasis +
      context fade) is removed. With the ruby line and authored per-word
      tiles the weight differentiation read as noise. isSalient still flows
      on blocks (semantics/telemetry) but changes nothing visually. The
      display box (hero-known / known-text) renders the whole known phrase
      at weight 600.

  script_toggle:
    composable: "composables/useScriptMode.ts (per-course localStorage)"
    semantics: |
      Native-script glyphs are ALWAYS the primary text on romanised courses.
      The toggle only shows/hides the romanisation as a ruby line (block.romanText)
      above each tile — a pronunciation crutch the learner drops as character
      recognition builds from exposure. Default ON (scriptMode 'roman').
    control: "ModeTray.vue — Aa (guide shown) / 文 (characters only)"
    prop: "LegoAssembly :show-romanization (LearningPlayer showRomanization computed)"

## ============================================================================
## LEARNING MODE — EASY / FAST (2026-08-06)
## ============================================================================

LEARNING_MODE:
  description: "The two learner-facing modes. Turbo was RETIRED 2026-08-06; there is exactly `easy` and `fast`."
  source: "packages/player-vue/src/components/LearningPlayer.vue (learningMode ref, isEasyMode computed, setLearningMode/toggleLearningMode) + components/PlayerRestingState.vue (the control)"

  modes:
    fast:
      default: "For EXISTING learners only — see default_by_history below."
      semantics: "Behaviourally identical to the pre-2026-08-06 `normal` mode — a learner who never touches the toggle sees no change at all. Keeps the baked belt speed ramp (getPlaybackSpeedMultiplier returns 1.0) and the global script_shape unchanged."
    easy:
      semantics: "Gentler. Six independent knobs — pause timing, phrase-count overlay, phrase-length cap, repeat count, repeated cycle types, BUILD filtering, and the known-side review pull filter — every one of them an algorithm_config row editable on the admin Speaking page. Nothing about Easy is hardcoded (Tom, 2026-08-07)."
      time: "Every pause term is 2x Fast (boot, assembly slope, floor, ceiling, multiplier) AND the belt taper is off (pause_belt_boot / pause_belt_assembly both 1.0 vs Fast's 0.8 assembly taper). Audio plays at native 1.0x — Easy cancels the beginner belt speed ramp, never speeds the voice past 1.0x."
      reps: "NO PHRASE-COUNT INFLATION (Tom's ruling, 2026-08-07: 'JUST DOUBLE'). Easy's per-mode `script_shape` overlay is EMPTY, so Easy generates exactly the global shape — the same phrase SET as Fast — and its extra repetition comes entirely from playing each cycle twice. Measured on fra_for_eng: 42 cycles per round against Fast's 22, i.e. ~2x. Easy previously ALSO doubled the four count knobs (14/4/24/6 against the global 7/2/12/3), which compounded with the doubling into a ~2.5x round nobody asked for — more DIFFERENT phrases, where what was asked for was the SAME phrase heard twice. The overlay REMAINS as the phrase-count multiplier, per mode, on the admin Speaking page; it simply defaults to no inflation. `spacedRepOffsets` is still never overridden: the Fibonacci ladder is WHEN a review fires, not how many reps it gives."
      length: "`maxPhraseLengthFraction: 0.5` — a CAP, not a sort direction. The ceiling is ABSOLUTE and COURSE-WIDE: 0.5 x the longest phrase in the whole course, measured in CHARACTERS of target text. Ordering stays shortest-first everywhere (so Fast, at 1.0 = uncapped, is provably byte-identical to before). Measured reasons for characters-not-syllables and course-wide-not-per-LEGO: target_syllable_count is NULL across whole courses and the syllable fallback returns 1 for non-Latin scripts, and BUILD pools average ~3.2 phrases so a per-LEGO ceiling never bit. A STARVATION GUARD keeps >=4 BUILD / >=5 USE per LEGO — phrase volume is a hard methodology rail, so the cap yields to it. Invalid/missing degrades to 1.0 (uncapped), never to a cap."
      repetition: "DOUBLING (Tom, 2026-08-07): every BUILD, REVIEW (spaced_rep), USE and CONSOLIDATE cycle plays EXACTLY TWICE, back to back — 'in EASY mode, double up every phrase, every BLD, every USE, every REVIEW, every CONSOLIDATE'. Never three times: 'a phrase repeated 3x would drive people nuts, but doubled up is perfect'. The INTRO cycle and the bare LEGO debut play ONCE — asked directly, Tom: 'of course not - the intro LEGO and not the LEGO alone'. FULLY PARAMETRISED (Tom, same night: 'ALL of this must be parametrised into the EASY settings in the app's configs — nothing hardcoded'): `phraseRepeatCount` (Easy 2, Fast 1) and `repeatedCycleTypes` (default build/spaced_rep/use) are both algorithm_config rows, editable per mode on the admin Speaking page. The ONE thing config cannot raise is the ceiling of 2 — normalizePhraseRepeatCount clamps a row asking for 3 and warns, because that is Tom's rule, not a preference. SEED-PHASE production reviews are never repeated whatever the type list says: that sandwich is already several cycles of one sentence. Implemented as a pure pass — providers/repeatPhraseCycles.ts — with TWO entry points that must stay in step: `repeatPhraseCycles` over the script walk's finished item list (AFTER the consecutive-duplicate removal, which would strip the twin, and BEFORE the A-64 floor, which allows at most two in a row and so guarantees doubled can never become tripled), and `repeatRoundCycles` inside backendCyclesToRounds for the INSTANT-PLAYBACK path. The second is not optional: INSTANT_PLAYBACK_ALL serves the first rounds of every session from the mode-blind cycles endpoint, so a repeat living only in the walk left the learner's first rounds undoubled and the doubling appearing mid-session. Each copy carries its own uuid/id (<original>_x2). A count of 1 returns the input untouched, so Fast stays byte-identical."
      build_phrases: "NO FILTERING ON BUILD (Tom, 2026-08-07, verbatim: 'no filtering on BLD phrases'). Easy passes filterBuildPhrases:false and its BUILD pools bypass the character cap entirely — build phrases are short by construction ('they usually only have one or two additional LEGOs from the previous material') and they ARE the debut round, so filtering them guts the round that exists to be generous. Fast keeps the historic filtered behaviour."
      review_pull_filter: "`reviewMaxKnownSyllables: 15` + `reviewSyllableFilterMaxRound: 100` (Tom, 2026-08-07). A PULL-TIME filter on which use phrase a REVIEW or CONSOLIDATE slot draws from a LEGO's basket — NOT a ceiling on the script. It counts the KNOWN language (the learner's own, the prompt side), not the target, using the canonical registry in @ssi/core/text (a verbatim port of ssi-dashboard-v7-clean/tools/lib/syllable-counters.cjs — a KNOWN, NAMED cross-repo duplication; changes must be mirrored, fixtures included). ACTIVE FOR ROUNDS 1-100 ONLY; from round 101 the basket opens fully, nothing backlogged, nothing cascading — per Tom, 'the whole idea of you have got a cascade, you have got a wall, once you get to 100 and 101, all these space repetitions is complete nonsense, makes no difference at all', because 'it's the LEGO that you are practicing' and every pulled phrase contains that LEGO whether or not the learner has met the phrase before. SHORTEST-IN-BASKET FALLBACK: a basket with nothing at or under the cap yields its shortest phrase, so a LEGO is never skipped and a review slot is never empty. INERT AND LOUD where the KNOWN language has no registered counter — one console warning per course plus `syllableCapApplied: false` on the generator result, never a silent no-op, which is exactly how the first syllable attempt failed. English is the known language of most courses and is registered. Debut BUILD/USE selection is deliberately NOT filtered. SUPERSEDES the absolute `maxPhraseSyllables: 20` target-side ceiling that shipped earlier the same night: that counted the wrong side of the pair, applied to the whole script rather than the pull, and never lifted. Both numbers are algorithm_config easy_mode DB rows, so retuning by ear is a Supabase edit, not a deploy."

  default_by_history:
    rule: "ASYMMETRIC (Aran via Tom, 2026-08-06). A learner with NO play history starts on EASY. A learner with ANY play history keeps TODAY'S behaviour — FAST — so nothing silently slows down mid-course for anyone already playing."
    why_module_default_stays_fast: "`DEFAULT_LEARNING_MODE` remains 'fast' deliberately: every pre-existing learner, and every path that reads the mode BEFORE progress resolves, must land on the unchanged experience. Easy is applied as a decision on top, never as the base."
    decision: "composables/newLearnerMode.ts — `resolveNewLearnerMode()`, a pure function returning the mode to apply or NULL for leave-alone. Returns null (not 'fast') on every leave-alone path so the caller never writes a mode it did not decide, which would clobber a choice still arriving from the learner row."
    gate: "Gated on `progressHistoryResolved` — before the saved-progress read returns, 'no history' and 'not loaded yet' are both empty; acting on the second would put an existing learner on Easy. A guest resolves TRUE synchronously (no server history by definition); a FAILED read never resolves."
    history_signals: "highest_completed_lego_id | last_completed_lego_id | highestCompletedRoundIndex !== null (round 0 is real play) | completedRounds > 0"
    explicit_choice_wins: "If the learner has ever chosen a mode (learner row OR localStorage), the default never fires — in either direction, forever."
    not_a_behavioural_signal: "Applied WITHOUT routing through setLearningMode(), so it emits no `learning_mode_toggle`: a silent default is not a learner judgement and must not enter the manual-dial evidence stream."
    status: "Tom has flagged this default as overridable by him; built as ruled, not yet challenged."

  control:
    surface: "Segmented Easy | Fast control on the RESTING SCREEN (PlayerRestingState.vue) — the mode you pick BEFORE you start, not a mid-session tray toggle. Rendered only when isPlayerReady; pointer-events re-enabled locally because .resting-state is pointer-events:none."
    wiring: "PlayerRestingState props :learning-mode, emits set-learning-mode -> LearningPlayer.setLearningMode(mode)"
    copy: "locales modes.easy / modes.fast / modes.learningModeLabel"
    not_in_tray: "ModeTray no longer carries a mode entry for this — the Turbo row and its explanation popup were deleted with Turbo."

  persistence:
    source_of_truth: "learner preferences `learning_mode: 'easy' | 'fast'` (optional; absent reads as 'fast') — replaced `turbo_mode_enabled`"
    fallback: "localStorage key `ssi-learning-mode` for signed-out learners; the learner row wins once auth resolves (watched)"

  config:
    rows: "algorithm_config `fast_mode` + `easy_mode` (was `normal_mode` + `turbo_boost`)"
    backward_compatible: "fast_mode falls back to the legacy normal_mode row; easy_mode falls back to DEFAULT_EASY layered over the tuned fast/normal values. A missing row can never break playback. The player writes no rows."

  timing_of_effect:
    immediate: "Pause / thinking time and playback speed — they go through SimplePlayer runtime overrides read fresh at every phase, so they land on the next cycle boundary."
    immediate_repeats: "DOUBLING TOO, since 2026-08-09. The reps are baked at generation time, but repetition is a purely ADDITIVE shape, so a toggle re-derives it from the queue the engine already holds — providers/reshapeRoundRepeats.ts strips every `_x2` copy and re-doubles under the new mode, with no query and no regeneration. setLearningMode splices the FORWARD rounds (replaceQueueFromCurrent, which keeps the playing round verbatim so cycleIndex cannot desync) and the runtime shouldSkipCycle gate drops any repeat copy still ahead of the cursor IN the playing round when the new mode's count is 1 — so Easy→Fast stops doubling on the very next cycle. Every mode WRITER goes through it: a watcher on learningMode covers the toggle, the cross-device preference landing after auth, and the new-learner default. THE CACHE TOO: getCachedScript is keyed on COURSE alone, so all three hydration sites (cold-start fast path, INF-PLAY warm path, course switch) reshape to the ACTIVE mode on read. Reproduced live by Tom 2026-08-09 — start Easy, flip to Fast mid-session, and Fast kept playing Easy's doubled phrases; the mode-blind cache meant a reload did not clear it either, so the old 'lands on the next build' promise never actually landed. Regression: providers/modeToggleMidSession.test.ts."
    next_script_build: "The phrase-length caps (the halved character fraction and the absolute syllable ceiling), the BUILD-phrase filter and the review syllable filter — those DROP items at generation time, so they cannot be restored from the loaded queue and still land on the next session or a course switch. No blocking mid-session regeneration by design. generateScript's dedupe key includes the mode so the other mode's cached walk is never served."

  analytics: "player_events `learning_mode_toggle` with payload { mode } (replaced `turbo_toggle`). Also fed to useBehaviouralEvidence as a MANUAL DIAL — the learner outranking the engine — which sets manualOverrideActive for adaptation v2 and never enters a unit's difficulty series."

  retired_with_turbo:
    cycle_cull: "`turboOmit` is GONE from ScriptItem, Cycle and toSimpleRounds — no learner gets a thinned-out round any more. SimplePlayer's generic shouldSkipCycle hook remains and is used by adaptation v2 alone."
    points_multiplier: "The turbo-usage session points multiplier is retired — sessionMultiplier is a flat 1.0, which is what every non-Turbo learner already got."
    pod_ratchet: "DELETED 2026-08-07. `usePodLapScheduler.skipAhead` — the pod-lap ratchet bump on skip — was Turbo's alone and had no caller left; the method and its test are gone. The pod counter now only ever advances by a PLAYED lap (markLapCompleted)."
    historical_events: "MANUAL_DIAL_EVENT_TYPES still contains 'turbo_toggle' ONLY so evidence replayed from pre-2026-08-06 sessions still counts as a manual dial. KEPT DELIBERATELY — dropping it would silently rewrite past behavioural evidence. The same argument keeps the 'turbo_toggle' count in the player_events rollup function in supabase/schema.sql."
    speed_override: "DELETED 2026-08-07. SimplePlayerRuntimeOverrides never regained a speed callback after Turbo went — playback rate comes from exactly ONE source, the baked `cycle.playbackSpeed`. The speedRampSync test block that guarded Turbo's target/baked round-trip is gone with it."
    config_row: "DELETED 2026-08-07. The `turbo_boost` row is removed from `algorithm_config`. NOT to be confused with `normal_mode`, which is LIVE — it is fast_mode's promotion-window fallback alias and must stay."
    learner_preference: "DELETED 2026-08-07. The dead `turbo_mode_enabled` key is stripped from every `learners.preferences` blob AND from the column DEFAULT, so new learners are not created with it again. It was false on all 1,092 learners carrying it, so the sweep is behaviourally a no-op."

## ============================================================================
## MODE TRAY (the mode button and its popup)
## ============================================================================

MODE_TRAY:
  source: "packages/player-vue/src/components/ModeTray.vue — rendered inside BottomNav.vue"
  rows: "Pronunciation guide | Listening mode | Offline mode (the Turbo row went with Turbo)"

  dismiss:
    behaviour: "A pointerdown ANYWHERE outside the tray closes it, as does Escape and the trigger button. Fixed 2026-08-07 — Tom reported ~10 times that the trigger was the only way out."
    where_it_lives: "A DOCUMENT-level pointerdown listener in capture phase, attached only while the tray is open, watching the .mode-tray-container root. Not the backdrop element."
    why_not_the_backdrop: "The .tray-backdrop is position:fixed;inset:0, which LOOKS full-screen and is not: .bottom-nav carries transform:translateX(-50%), and a transformed ancestor is the containing block for fixed descendants — so the backdrop measures 358x72px at the nav pill in an 844px-tall viewport (measured live, e2e/mode-tray-outside-tap-probe.mjs). It could only ever dismiss taps already on the nav. The backdrop is now purely the dim, never the dismiss."
    why_not_teleport: "Teleporting the backdrop to <body> to escape that containing block is exactly what 1c41b1d2 (2026-07-16) REMOVED: in embedded play (.player-container.is-teach-embedded, also transformed) it rejoins the root stacking context, paints above the locally z-indexed tray and eats every row tap. That fix restored the rows and left the outside-tap dismiss dead — which is the bug this replaces. A document listener sidesteps the containing-block question entirely and can swallow nothing."
    no_reopen_race: "The trigger button is INSIDE the watched root, so its pointerdown is ignored and only its click toggles — outside-close can never fire and let the trigger immediately reopen."
    click_swallow: "After an outside pointerdown dismisses the tray, the click that same press produces is swallowed once (capture-phase listener, scoped to the element that press landed on, 700ms self-cancel) so dismissing never doubles as a tap on the transport underneath. If no click follows — a scroll or a drag — the arming expires and eats nothing."
    tests: "src/components/ModeTray.outsideTap.test.ts (8 unit tests) + e2e/mode-tray-outside-tap-probe.mjs (real browser against a real build; prints the backdrop's measured geometry as the evidence)."

## ============================================================================
## LISTENING OVERLAY (headphones mode)
## ============================================================================

LISTENING_OVERLAY:
  source: "packages/player-vue/src/components/ListeningOverlay.vue"
  entry: |
    The mode tray (ModeTray.vue) ONLY — a "Listening mode" on/off row sitting
    between Pronunciation guide and Offline mode, ungated. Tom's ruling
    (2026-08-06, 22:38Z): listening mode belongs in the place learners already
    knew, and Settings is the WRONG home for it — so the Settings → Tools row
    that briefly held it, and its openListening emit, are GONE. The row emits
    toggleListening → BottomNav → PlayerContainer.handleToggleListening →
    LearningPlayer.handleListeningToggle, which opens when closed and closes
    when open, so one row is the whole round trip. The transport back/play
    button (BottomNav exitListeningMode) is unchanged as the other way out.
    Full detail: apml/interfaces/listening-mode.apml ENTRY_POINT.
  tabs: |
    Dialogues | Core | All — in that order, Dialogues is the DEFAULT view
    (Tom 2026-06-07). Dialogues = Layer 2 pod scenes (useListeningPods,
    grouped by listening_pod_sentences.scene_number); Core = every seed
    sentence; All = every USE phrase. Phrase data loads lazily on first
    tap of All/Core — the default mount only loads the scene list.
  dialogues_playlist: |
    Scenes play as ONE continuous playlist: scene-end segues into the next
    scene automatically, and after the last scene wraps around to the
    first (Spotify playlist loop). The scene-loop button (visible with a
    scene open) is single-track repeat: ON = restart the current scene
    forever; default OFF. Scene matching is by sceneNumber — PodScene has
    no `id` field (a former `s.id === selected.id` undefined comparison
    made every scene "advance" to scene 2).
  background_lock_protocol: |
    (2026-06-07, extended 2026-06-08) The overlay follows the SAME bg/lock
    rules as the main flow, INF PLAY and pod laps, on three axes:
    1. GAPS = audio, not timers. Every playback gap advances on an audio
       'ended' event, NEVER a bare setTimeout (iOS freezes timers on a
       backgrounded/locked tab). The 50ms intra-turn and 800ms inter-turn
       gaps play as genuinely-silent one-shot WAV clips on the same
       element (ListeningAudioController.playSilence → playback/silentWav.ts,
       the builder shared with SimplePlayer's PAUSE fix). playbackRate
       applies, so faster speeds tighten gaps proportionally.
    2. BYTES = cached WAV, not streamed mp3. Play URLs resolve through the
       SHARED substrate resolveCachedPlaybackUrl(audioCache, id, proxyUrl):
       a WAV blob decoded from IndexedDB when present (real PCM, no network
       — WebKit plays it locked), else the proxy URL (instant cold-cache
       first play). This is the ONE definition both the overlay and the
       main 4-phase cycle (SimplePlayer.resolveAudioUrl) play through. To
       make the cache HIT ahead of a locked clip, prefetch warms IndexedDB
       via audioCache.persistent.ensure (warmClip) — top-5 rows on scene
       open, a rolling +2 warm-ahead each turn, and the next scene's head
       during the last turn — NOT just the SW fetch (which streams under a
       Range request iOS stalls on when locked).
    3. SESSION = live. mediaSession.setPositionState updates on the
       element's timeupdate (ListeningAudioController) so iOS/Android see
       an advancing session and are less likely to suspend the tab —
       same heuristic as SimplePlayer.updateMediaPositionState.
    The 15s safety timeout inside play() remains a foreground fallback
    only. RULE for any new playback surface: a gap is "a phase with
    audio" not "a phase with only a timer", AND bytes reach the speaker
    via the shared cached-WAV resolver, never a raw streamed URL.
    CONVERGENCE: the overlay does NOT reuse SimplePlayer's 4-phase CYCLE
    semantics (it's a flat clip list) — it shares the audio SUBSTRATE
    (resolver + cache + position + silent-gap advance). One bytes-to-
    speaker path, two callers.

  speaker_colouring: |
    (Tom 2026-06-07) The voice-casting 4-colour-map principle, made
    VISIBLE: useListeningPods builds the pod-wide "shares a scene" graph
    and greedy-colours speakers in first-appearance order (same algorithm
    as Popty's voice colouring proof — never needed >4 colours on real
    pods). One character = one colour across the whole pod; scene-mates
    never share. SPEAKER_PALETTE (6 mid-tone colours) exported from the
    composable. Rendered as: an uppercase speaker chip + dot above every
    teleprompter dialogue row (.phrase-speaker), and cast dots on each
    scene card (.scene-card-cast). Colour rides turn.colorIndex; display
    name is the speaker label minus its time/place annotation.

  scene_granularity: |
    One scene = one dialogue beat. Montage pods (several mini-dialogues
    sharing a backdrop, e.g. fra pod-0's "day of greetings") must be
    SPLIT into one scene per beat in the data — same-speaker turn merging
    (useListeningPods.mergeTurns) operates within a scene and will glue
    the tail of one beat to the head of the next if they share a
    scene_number (the 2026-06-07 French bug: "Have a good one! Excuse me,
    is this seat taken?" as one card).

